This C program uses the pigpio library to control a 28BYJ-48 unipolar stepper motor through an L293D motor driver. It simulates an automatic door lock with full-step (single-coil) excitation. The program drives the coils on BCM GPIO4, GPIO17, GPIO27, and GPIO22 (mapped to L293D inputs IN1-IN4) with a 3 ms inter-step delay. Two momentary push-buttons use internal pull-ups: LOCK on BCM GPIO24 and UNLOCK on BCM GPIO23 (active-low). Pressing LOCK rotates the motor 512 steps clockwise (about a quarter turn); pressing UNLOCK rotates 512 steps counter-clockwise. The program checks the return value of `gpioInitialise()` and reports an error on failure, and checks every `gpioWrite`, `gpioSetMode`, and `gpioSetPullUpDown` return code. It de-energizes all coils after each rotation completes to reduce power consumption and motor heating. The program handles `SIGINT` by de-energizing the coils and calling `gpioTerminate()`. Debounce button reads with a short delay so a single press does not trigger multiple rotations, and prints clear console messages for lock and unlock actions and for errors.

